{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794802066914,"sessionID":"ses_e1d81443fffeGbe5CDTKaTeXws","part":{"id":"prt_1e28a25e2001FCkRGSohs9L101","sessionID":"ses_e1d81443fffeGbe5CDTKaTeXws","messageID":"msg_1e28a1d00001wsSjEYIINlL2mh","type":"step-start","snapshot":"c208f27458053d25b8a84c07d9e6bbc004b6d4e1"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794802090288,"sessionID":"ses_e1d81443fffeGbe5CDTKaTeXws","part":{"partID":"prt_1e28a4c83001Ze57oSAKmeJTNf","sessionID":"ses_e1d81443fffeGbe5CDTKaTeXws","messageID":"msg_1e28a1d00001wsSjEYIINlL2mh","type":"tool","id":"call_1353cdf027dc4ba785fa74b6","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 6: What \"capped\" means per runtime, and the row the scorer cannot see at all\n\n## Focus\n\nThe strategy's recommended focus was an audit of the six inherited `limit:` lines. One of them names the property this lane exists to check — that a row which moves HEAD cannot be measured — and iteration 5's start audit showed that `capped` decides which start rows are judgeable. So this iteration traced `capped` from the runner's own records through the scorer, and checked whether a restore failure reaches the score at all.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n1. Read the scoring contract that decides whether a run is scored at all.\n2. Read the `capped` definition.\n3. Counted exit codes and restore flags per runtime over the deduplicated run lines.\n4. Read the start-row exclusion rule that consumes `capped`.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. The scorer never reads `restored` or `headMoved`; a run is scored whenever its session loads, \"whatever exit the turn ended with\", and the exit code itself never decides a score. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1998-1999]\n\n2. `capped` is true for two different events: an exit code of 143, or an exit code of `null`. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:2035-2038]\n\n3. The two causes split cleanly by runtime in the 033 arm. OpenCode's 26 capped lines are all exit 143 (15 off, 11 on). Devin's 19 capped lines are all exit `null` (10 off, 9 on). Claude has none. Pi has 2 off and 0 on, both 143. DSH has none. [INFERENCE: counted over the deduplicated lines of `scratch/runs/{claude,opencode,pi,devin,dsh}.jsonl`] So the same printed word describes a wall-clock cap on OpenCode and something else entirely on Devin, and a reader comparing the two runtimes' `capped` columns is comparing unlike events.\n\n4. Devin's exit-null lines are mostly not restore faults either. All 19 carry `restored: true`, none carries `headMoved`, and the sampled ones have no changed paths; only 2 of the 19 are the `fresh-skill-04` restore-failed lines the runner's rerun log describes. [INFERENCE: counted over the deduplicated devin lines, joined against `scratch/reruns.txt`] The missing exit code alone is enough to label a turn capped.\n\n5. A capped start row is dropped from the start rate's denominator and counted apart instead. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:2376-2383] Devin's printed start rate therefore rests on 11 and 12 judgeable rows out of 15, not because the turns were slow but because their exit code was never recorded — the wall-clock rule applied to a different fault.\n\n6. The row the runner calls unmeasurable is scored as an ordinary row. DSH's restore-failed `fresh-skill-04` line carries exit 0, so it passes every filter and is counted in the DSH block; `reruns.txt` records the same row as \"the row is not rerun because the same row moved head on devin twice\" and calls it unmeasurable there. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/reruns.txt] Nothing in the scorer distinguishes a turn that ran from a turn that rebased the arena.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Reading the `capped` column as a wall-clock count on every runtime. On devin it is a missing-exit-code count.\n- Treating the runner's `restore_failed` verdict as reaching the score. It does not: only `exit` is consulted.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Searching for a scored-row field that records the restore fault. `restored` and `headMoved` exist on the run line and are never read by the scorer.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: the audit's scope. The strategy asked for all six limit lines; this iteration traced the two it could test from records (the head-move line and the capped-turn line) and left the Claude `CLAUDE.md` line and the off-arm replay line to the next pass rather than assert them unverified.\n- Contradictory evidence: the runner's rerun log and the summary's `unscored: 0` disagree about whether a restore-failed row is measurable. Both are recorded; the scorer's behaviour is the one that decides printed numbers.\n- Missing dependencies: the devin CLI's exit-code capture was not traced to its source, so the cause of the 19 null exits is stated as unknown rather than attributed.\n- Partial success: the exit-code and restore counts are complete for the five logs; the dsh line's contribution to DSH's followed rate was not recomputed.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1998-2040`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:2376-2383`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1905-1916`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/reruns.txt`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/opencode.jsonl`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/devin.jsonl`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/dsh.jsonl`\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.85\n- Questions addressed: (e) follow-on, measurement integrity of the capped column\n- Questions answered: none as tracked; this supplies the audit the rank needs\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: joining the scorer's field rule to the per-runtime exit-code census produced a split no summary prints. The word \"capped\" looked like one quantity in eleven printed blocks; the census shows it is a wall-clock cap on one runtime and a missing exit code on another.\n- What did not work and why: the first hypothesis was that every devin exit-null line was a restore fault. Checking `restored` on all 19 refuted it, since 17 of them are restored normally; the finding had to narrow to the missing exit code, which is the actual predicate.\n- What I would do differently: read the scoring contract's treatment of `exit` before reading any `capped` column. The definition is four lines of code and it changes every cross-runtime comparison built on that column.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nClose the two limit lines this pass left unverified — the off-arm replay line and Claude's `CLAUDE.md` read — then check whether the paired-row churn per runtime (devin 3 gained and 3 lost, pi 8 and 7) sits in one row type, which is the last follow-through question the lane has not touched.\n","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/cross-cli-5/iterations/iteration-006.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/cross-cli-5/iterations/iteration-006.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/cross-cli-5/iterations/iteration-006.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\":\"# Iteration 6: What \\\"capped\\\" means per runtime, and the row the scorer cannot see at all\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nThe strategy's recommended focus was an audit of the six inherited `limit:` lines. One of them names the property this lane exists to check — that a row which moves HEAD cannot be measured — and iteration 5's start audit showed that `capped` decides which start rows are judgeable. So this iteration traced `capped` from the runner's own records through the scorer, and checked whether a restore failure reaches the score at all.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n1. Read the scoring contract that decides whether a run is scored at all.\\n2. Read the `capped` definition.\\n3. Counted exit codes and restore flags per runtime over the deduplicated run lines.\\n4. Read the start-row exclusion rule that consumes `capped`.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. The scorer never reads `restored` or `headMoved`; a run is scored whenever its session loads, \\\"whatever exit the turn ended with\\\", and the exit code itself never decides a score. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1998-1999]\\n\\n2. `capped` is true for two different events: an exit code of 143, or an exit code of `null`. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:2035-2038]\\n\\n3. The two causes split cleanly by runtime in the 033 arm. OpenCode's 26 capped lines are all exit 143 (15 off, 11 on). Devin's 19 capped lines are all exit `null` (10 off, 9 on). Claude has none. Pi has 2 off and 0 on, both 143. DSH has none. [INFERENCE: counted over the deduplicated lines of `scratch/runs/{claude,opencode,pi,devin,dsh}.jsonl`] So the same printed word describes a wall-clock cap on OpenCode and something else entirely on Devin, and a reader comparing the two runtimes' `capped` columns is comparing unlike events.\\n\\n4. Devin's exit-null lines are mostly not restore faults either. All 19 carry `restored: true`, none carries `headMoved`, and the sampled ones have no changed paths; only 2 of the 19 are the `fresh-skill-04` restore-failed lines the runner's rerun log describes. [INFERENCE: counted over the deduplicated devin lines, joined against `scratch/reruns.txt`] The missing exit code alone is enough to label a turn capped.\\n\\n5. A capped start row is dropped from the start rate's denominator and counted apart instead. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:2376-2383] Devin's printed start rate therefore rests on 11 and 12 judgeable rows out of 15, not because the turns were slow but because their exit code was never recorded — the wall-clock rule applied to a different fault.\\n\\n6. The row the runner calls unmeasurable is scored as an ordinary row. DSH's restore-failed `fresh-skill-04` line carries exit 0, so it passes every filter and is counted in the DSH block; `reruns.txt` records the same row as \\\"the row is not rerun because the same row moved head on devin twice\\\" and calls it unmeasurable there. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/reruns.txt] Nothing in the scorer distinguishes a turn that ran from a turn that rebased the arena.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Reading the `capped` column as a wall-clock count on every runtime. On devin it is a missing-exit-code count.\\n- Treating the runner's `restore_failed` verdict as reaching the score. It does not: only `exit` is consulted.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Searching for a scored-row field that records the restore fault. `restored` and `headMoved` exist on the run line and are never read by the scorer.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: the audit's scope. The strategy asked for all six limit lines; this iteration traced the two it could test from records (the head-move line and the capped-turn line) and left the Claude `CLAUDE.md` line and the off-arm replay line to the next pass rather than assert them unverified.\\n- Contradictory evidence: the runner's rerun log and the summary's `unscored: 0` disagree about whether a restore-failed row is measurable. Both are recorded; the scorer's behaviour is the one that decides printed numbers.\\n- Missing dependencies: the devin CLI's exit-code capture was not traced to its source, so the cause of the 19 null exits is stated as unknown rather than attributed.\\n- Partial success: the exit-code and restore counts are complete for the five logs; the dsh line's contribution to DSH's followed rate was not recomputed.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1998-2040`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:2376-2383`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1905-1916`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/reruns.txt`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/opencode.jsonl`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/devin.jsonl`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/033-untuned-arm-arena-run/scratch/runs/dsh.jsonl`\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 0.85\\n- Questions addressed: (e) follow-on, measurement integrity of the capped column\\n- Questions answered: none as tracked; this supplies the audit the rank needs\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: joining the scorer's field rule to the per-runtime exit-code census produced a split no summary prints. The word \\\"capped\\\" looked like one quantity in eleven printed blocks; the census shows it is a wall-clock cap on one runtime and a missing exit code on another.\\n- What did not work and why: the first hypothesis was that every devin exit-null line was a restore fault. Checking `restored` on all 19 refuted it, since 17 of them are restored normally; the finding had to narrow to the missing exit code, which is the actual predicate.\\n- What I would do differently: read the scoring contract's treatment of `exit` before reading any `capped` column. The definition is four lines of code and it changes every cross-runtime comparison built on that column.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nClose the two limit lines this pass left unverified — the off-arm replay line and Claude's `CLAUDE.md` read — then check whether the paired-row churn per runtime (devin 3 gained and 3 lost, pi 8 and 7) sits in one row type, which is the last follow-through question the lane has not touched.\\n\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/cross-cli-5/iterations/iteration-006.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794802076803,"end":1794802090288}}}}
